Let $g\in\mathbb{N}_0$ be given.
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item There exist smoothly embedded genus $g$ orientable surfaces with normal Euler number~$1$ in $\mathbb{RP}^4\#\mathbb{CP}^2$ that are topologically but not smoothly equivalent. Moreover, they are not smoothly equivalent even after any number of internal and external stabilisations.
			\item For $n\in\mathbb{N}$ large enough, there exist smoothly embedded genus $g$ orientable surfaces with normal Euler number $0$ in $(\mathbb{RP}^2\times S^2)\#n(S^2\times S^2)$ that are topologically but not smoothly equivalent. Moreover, they are not smoothly equivalent even after any number of internal and external stabilisations.
		\end{enumerate}

\leavevmode
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item[(i)] Since any pair of orientable, homeomorphic $4$-manifolds are stably diffeomorphic by a result of Gompf \cite{Gompf}, the ambient manifold in our examples is necessarily non-orientable. For more details on stably exotic $4$-manifolds see \cite{KP-stablyexotic} for the closed case and \cite{KOPR} for the case with boundary. In particular one can use \cite[Proposition~8.3]{KOPR} to show that our examples have exteriors that remain stably exotic after a connected sum with any number of copies of $S^1\times S^3$, thus providing an infinite family of such pairs. On the other hand for any three topologically equivalent surfaces in a given $4$-manifold at least two of them become smoothly equivalent after some number of external stabilisations as can be seen from \cite[Proposition~8.1]{KOPR}.
			\item[(ii)] The existence of stably exotic knotted surfaces in terms of isotopy, i.e.\ topologically isotopic but not smoothly stably isotopic surfaces, remains open \cite[Question~1.1]{GOP25}. For such examples to exist, the ambient manifold cannot be simply connected by \cite[Theorem 1.2]{Galvin24} and further restrictions on the fundamental group are given by \cite[Theorem~A]{GOP25}.
		\end{enumerate}
